{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JCLcEQ466zdt3bH6D4scTi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Components:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of utilities\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications\n\n**Current Implementation State**: The testutils, sys, and format packages are complete with their core functionality and tests. The cat and wc utilities have been implemented, though wc's implementation appears to be cut off in the source listing.\n\n**Key Discrepancy**: The implementation_status document claims no code exists, but the actual source_code field shows multiple working Go files across these packages. I'm trusting the source code as the authoritative state.\n\n**Release Status**: Looking at the code_status for rel00.0, the magefiles specification is marked complete but code hasn't started yet. Looking at the release roadmap, most of the earlier batches already have implementations in place based on the source code. The sponge utility in rel01.2 is the first one that's actually missing code, even though the spec is complete. After that, rel01.3 for du also lacks an implementation, and then rel02.0 through rel02.2 are all still pending their specs. I can see the code status shows sponge is at rel01.2 with no code started yet on this branch, and there's no source code for it in the project context. Looking at the existing PRDs referenced in the codebase, I notice sponge doesn't have a corresponding PRD file listed, though the analysis mentions an orphaned prd019 that might be related.\n\nThe constraint requires anchoring tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs, but I don't have the sponge PRD content available in the project context to reference those IDs directly. I know the spec is marked complete and it's a moreutils tool scheduled for release 01.2, but without the actual PRD content, I'm limited in how specifically I can tie implementation tasks to documented requirements.\n\nLooking at what's already been implemented across the releases, I notice the existing packages and commands have test coverage, though I'm seeing some gaps—for instance, the format package doesn't appear to have test files yet despite being in the completed release 00.0.\n\nThe earliest release without content is 01.2 with sponge, but I don't have the PRD for that. I should double-check the code_status details to see what's actually marked as incomplete versus what's already been started. Looking at the remaining items, I see that trivial utilities, tee, and head all lack specs and code entirely. For the magefiles work in rel00.0-uc001, the constraint excludes it from task generation anyway, and I don't have enough detail about cmd/version to create a proper task. So the earliest concrete gap is the missing tests for pkg/format in rel00.0.\n\nuc002 — columns.go and size.go exist in the source but have no corresponding test files. I need to find the PRD requirement IDs to properly scope this work.\n\nLooking at the code comments, columns.go implements prd003-format requirements R1.1 through R1.4 (column distribution, padding operations, and ordering), while size.go covers R3.1 through R3.4. This gives me enough to propose a test task that covers both the column and sizing functionality.\n\nSince I'm limited to one task and each task can contain at most 4 requirements, I'll focus on the column tests first—that's R1.1 through R1.4, which covers the core formatting logic for distributing and padding columns. That's exactly at the limit, so it works well as a focused test task.\n\nBut I'm second-guessing myself on priority. The sponge implementation in rel01.2 would move the project forward more, yet rel00.0 is the earliest incomplete release and the format code already exists—it just needs tests. The code_status table shows format as \"not started,\" but the source code clearly exists, so the real gap is test coverage. Given P1's directive to focus on the earliest incomplete release, rel00.0 should take precedence, even though rel01.2 might feel more impactful. So rel00.0 is actually complete for generation purposes since magefiles is excluded—the only real gap is format tests. I'm setting up the test file for pkg/format columns with the implementation file and PRD as reference material to understand what needs testing.\n\nThe test requirements cover the core functionality: verifying the Columns function handles entries within terminal width, testing the padding functions work correctly with both ASCII and multi-byte runes, checking that entries are ordered column-major, and handling edge cases like empty input or oversized entries. I'll follow the existing test patterns in the codebase and use table-driven tests, making sure to account for the 2-space column gap constant.\n\nNow I'm looking at the line budget for the test file—existing test files in the project range from 57 to 304 lines, so fitting tests for Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, and potentially HumanSize in around 150-200 lines seems feasible, though I need to check if I can keep it within the 4-requirement constraint.\n\nLet me organize this into four focused requirements: testing the Columns function, testing the padding functions together, testing HumanSize with both binary and SI formatting, and testing edge cases like zero bytes. I'll create two new test files—one for columns and one for size—which keeps me well within the file limits.\n\nNow I'm drafting the task title to include the requirement IDs as specified, settling on \"Test prd003-format R1 (columns/padding) and R3 (human-readable sizes)\" and building out the full task definition with the necessary reading materials and implementation details. - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml (PRD with R1 and R3 requirements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/columns_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for Columns, PadRight, PadLeft\n      - path: pkg/format/size_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for HumanSize\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: Test Columns function distributes entries into column-major grid fitting within terminal width (prd003-format R1.1, R1.4), including edge cases for empty input, single entry, and entries wider than terminal width.\n      - id: R2\n        text: Test PadRight and PadLeft pad strings using rune count, not byte count, handling both ASCII and multi-byte UTF-8 strings (prd003-format R1.2, R1.3).\n      - id: R3\n        text: Test HumanSize converts byte counts to human-readable strings in both binary (1024-based, K/M/G/T/P/E) and SI (1000-based, kB/MB/GB/TB) unit systems, with one decimal place below 10 at chosen unit and no decimal at 10 or above (prd003-format R3.1, R3.2, R3.3).\n      - id: R4\n        text: Test HumanSize returns \"0\" for zero bytes and formats values below the first unit threshold as plain integers (prd003-format R3.4).\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Use table-driven tests with named sub-tests via t.Run to organize test cases clearly and enable selective test execution.\n      - id: D2\n        text: The columnGap constant is 2 spaces between adjacent columns (defined in columns.go). Tests must account for this gap when computing expected column layouts.\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Copyright header: // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved. followed by // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT, matching all existing source files.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: pkg/format/columns_test.go and pkg/format/size_test.go compile with no errors.\n      - id: AC2\n        text: go test ./pkg/format/... passes with all tests green.\n      - id: AC3\n        text: All test cases cover the requirements and edge cases specified in R1–R4.\n\nI'm organizing the test structure with table-driven tests using t.Run to match the existing test patterns in the codebase, and I need to ensure the copyright headers and the 2-space column gap are properly accounted for in the expected outputs. Everything checks out against the constraints—the task is focused, requirements are minimal, the code footprint stays well under the limit, and it's properly anchored to the PRD with appropriate file naming conventions. I'm ready to write the final response.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52624,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52624},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12899080-29d5-40fe-b1db-48df5215b816","uuid":"04a0cfa3-e76b-441b-b4d7-b928cd871260"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JCLcEQ466zdt3bH6D4scTi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation per DD7), `docs/` (human-authored specifications).\n\n**Current Implementation** (from source_code, which is authoritative over prose):\n- `pkg/testutils/` — code and tests exist\n- `pkg/sys/` — code and tests exist (signal, stat, terminal)\n- `pkg/format/` — code exists (columns.go, size.go) but **no tests**\n- `cmd/wc/` — code and differential tests exist\n- `cmd/cat/` — code and differential tests exist\n- `cmd/sponge/` — no code on this branch\n- `cmd/du/` — no code on this branch\n\n**Current Release**: rel00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure) is the earliest incomplete release. Three of four use cases have code and tests (magefiles is excluded from generation per DD7, testutils and sys are complete). The gap is **pkg/format has no tests** (uc002-format).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nPer P1 (release-driven priority), rel00.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within rel00.0:\n- uc001-magefiles: excluded from generation (DD7)\n- uc002-format: code exists, **tests missing** — this is the gap\n- uc003-sys: code and tests exist\n- uc004-testutils: code and tests exist\n\nThe single highest-priority task is writing tests for `pkg/format` to close rel00.0. This unblocks rel01.2 (sponge) and beyond.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Test prd003-format R1 (columns/padding) and R3 (human-readable sizes)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - pkg/format/columns.go (Columns, PadRight, PadLeft implementations to test)\n      - pkg/format/size.go (HumanSize implementation to test)\n      - pkg/format/doc.go (package documentation referencing prd003-format R1, R3)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml (PRD with R1 and R3 requirements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/columns_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for Columns, PadRight, PadLeft\n      - path: pkg/format/size_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for HumanSize\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: Test Columns function distributes entries into a column-major grid fitting within the given terminal width (prd003-format R1.1, R1.4), including edge cases for empty input (returns nil), single entry, all entries fitting in one row, and entries wider than terminal width forcing single-column layout.\n      - id: R2\n        text: Test PadRight and PadLeft pad strings to the specified display width using rune count not byte count (prd003-format R1.2, R1.3), verifying correct behavior with ASCII strings, multi-byte UTF-8 strings, and strings already at or exceeding the target width (returned unchanged).\n      - id: R3\n        text: Test HumanSize converts byte counts to human-readable strings in both binary (1024-based with K/M/G/T/P/E suffixes) and SI (1000-based with kB/MB/GB/TB suffixes) unit systems, with one decimal place for values below 10 at the chosen unit and no decimal for values at 10 or above (prd003-format R3.1, R3.2, R3.3).\n      - id: R4\n        text: Test HumanSize returns \"0\" for zero bytes and formats values below the first unit threshold as plain integers without suffix (prd003-format R3.4).\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Use table-driven tests with named sub-tests via t.Run, matching the project test style in pkg/sys/stat_test.go and pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go.\n      - id: D2\n        text: The columnGap constant is 2 spaces between adjacent columns (columns.go line 12). Tests must account for this gap when computing expected column layouts.\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Copyright header on every file: '// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.' followed by '// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT', matching all existing source files in the project.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: pkg/format/columns_test.go and pkg/format/size_test.go compile with no errors.\n      - id: AC2\n        text: go test ./pkg/format/... passes with all tests green.\n      - id: AC3\n        text: Tests cover all four exported functions -- Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, and HumanSize -- with both normal inputs and edge cases.\n      - id: AC4\n        text: Columns tests verify column-major ordering (entries fill down the first column then the second) by checking the row contents returned for a known input set.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52624,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52624},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12899080-29d5-40fe-b1db-48df5215b816","uuid":"45111a52-6390-451e-aa59-67e9ebb05ddc"}
